 but said  he began to deteriorate several days ago  his family thanked them for offering the  experimental transplant they hope it  will help future research  ahead as countries ban russian oil  environmentalists make a push to turn to  greener energy for good  [Music]  canada the u.s and the european union  have committed to reducing or completely  banning the import of russian oil and  gas there is going to be an immediate  need to replace that supply but there's  also pressure to use this moment to push  for a faster transition to green energy  ann gaviola explains  environmental lawyer phil puffin has  been a longtime advocate for a shift  away from car culture as if we needed  another wake-up call the pandemic shift  to remote work and virtual meetings have  some people rethinking daily commutes  and in-person business trips  and now with war in ukraine forcing a  re-evaluation of the world's reliance on  russia he thinks this could be steps  towards a larger shift there's a  confluence of things that are happening  all at once that are going to cause this  change to stick this time  global gas prices have soared above a  hundred dollars a barrel for the first  time since 2014. tuesday on the heels of  the u.s ban on russian oil russia warned  a european ban could lead to an  unpredictable surge and that oil at  three hundred dollars a barrel or more  could be in the making but what this  conflict clearly shows is how dangerous  our dependence on a fossil fuel system  is  analysts say an energy inflation spiral  could squeeze consumers and businesses  and push europe latin america and  eventually north america and china into  a recession  the alberta regional director for the  pembina institute says canada needs to  urgently address what he calls the  climate crisis or we risk falling  further behind countries like china in  the global green shift the amount of  renewable energy production that's  happening there and electric vehicles is  really dramatic  they're becoming a global leader in  these technologies expanding our  reliance on oil and gas increases our  vulnerability it accelerates climate  change the fires and the floods that  were that are already costing us  billions and and displacing millions of  people  worldwide environmentalists say there's  a strong strategic moral and business  case for moving away from fossil fuel  dependence  anne gaviola global news toronto  patience has paid off for a team  searching for one of history's greatest  shipwrecks explorer ernest shackleton's  ship endurance has been found beneath  antarctic ice 106 years after being  crushed by ice an expedition spent weeks  searching the remote location and made  the discovery using state-of-the-art  underwater technology it is 3 000 meters  below the surface upright and according  to the team's director in remarkably  good shape  wood is is as fresh as the day the ship  went down you can see her paint work you  can see the bolts you can see the ship's  name you know endurance curved across  her stern and beneath it the great  five-pointed star  the ship sank in 1915 forcing shackleton  and his 27-man crew to escape on foot  and in small boats it was a 10-month  ordeal to safety he was trying to become  the first to cross antarctica via the  south pole he failed but his journey is  still considered one of the most epic  feats of endurance in antarctic  exploration  endurance is now protected as an  historic site under the antarctic treaty  next how the war in ukraine could make a  global hunger crisis even worse  russia's attack on ukraine is taking  place in a region often called the bread  basket of europe both countries produce  grain that many other countries rely on  especially in the middle east and north  africa redmond shannon explains what the  war if it's prolonged could do to food  supplies for millions of people  ukrainian refugees have fled often  without knowing where their next meal  will come from  inside the country's besieged cities  food is running out a tragic irony in a  part of the world long known as the  bread basket of europe ukraine and  russia are huge producers and exporters  of grains and oil seeds but many  ukrainian farmers have left the fields  to fight and black sea ports are closed  right now knowing how much black sea  actually matters in the scale of the  international grain export it's it's  going to be very very and very bad the  war has strangled supply with the price  of wheat and sunflower seeds breaking  records about 80 percent of sunflower  oil  comes from these two countries  so  the the impact of this crisis is  worldwide this is a disaster for  struggling countries close to ukraine  lebanon where the economy is already in  tatters gets most of its wheat from  there war-torn yemen where hunger is  already a huge problem relies heavily on  affordable grain imports the ripple  effects could soon be felt even more  strongly according to this ukrainian  trade expert it's not just some local  ukrainian russian war and if you don't  have another refugee crisis from those  areas which which will be under fed  this is something that the world needs  to do we need the way of ending this the  world food programme says the war  compounds the effect of covet 19 climate  change and other conflicts it says 44  million people are already on the edge  of famine and that could rise further  unless it gets more funds better still  would be an end to this and other wars  redmond shannon global news london  that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen in ukraine's  capital city today an orchestra  assembled for an open-air concert we  leave you tonight with some of their  music  the kiev classical symphony orchestra  set up in medan square and among the  songs they played was ukraine's national  anthem the lyrics translated to english  say the glory and freedom of ukraine has  not yet perished our enemies will vanish  like dew in the sun  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow  [Music]  so  you
 

